      <h1 id="virt-controlling-vm-states" class="group scroll-mt-20">Control virtual machine states<a href="#virt-controlling-vm-states" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h1>
      <p class="_abstract">You can use <code>virtctl</code> to manage virtual machine states and perform other actions from the CLI. For example, you can use <code>virtctl</code> to force stop a VM or expose a port.</p>
      <p>You can stop, start, restart, reset, pause, and unpause virtual machines from the web console.</p>
      <h2 id="virt-configure-rbac-console-subresources-api_virt-controlling-vm-states" class="group scroll-mt-20">Configuring RBAC permissions for managing VM states by using the web console<a href="#virt-configure-rbac-console-subresources-api_virt-controlling-vm-states" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">To allow users to manage virtual machine (VM) states by using the OpenShift Container Platform web console, you must create an RBAC cluster role and cluster role binding. The cluster role uses the <code>subresources.kubevirt.io</code> API to define which resources can be controlled by certain users or groups.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You have cluster administrator access to an OpenShift Container Platform cluster where OpenShift Virtualization is installed.</li>
      <li>You have installed the OpenShift CLI (<code>oc</code>).</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create a <code>ClusterRole</code> object that allows the target user or group to manage VM states:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\nkind: ClusterRole\nmetadata:\n  name: vm-manager-access\nrules:\n  - apiGroups:\n      - subresources.kubevirt.io\n    resources:\n      - virtualmachines/start\n      - virtualmachines/stop\n    verbs:\n      - update\n# ..."} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Run the following command to apply the cluster role:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f <filename>.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Confirm that the cluster role was created by running the following command and observing the output:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get clusterrole <name>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Example output:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                AGE\nvm-manager-access   15s"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Inspect the details of the cluster role, and ensure the intended rules for <code>subresources.kubevirt.io</code> are present, specifically the <code>virtualmachines/start</code> and <code>virtualmachines/stop</code> subresources.
      <p>Run the following command and observe the output:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc describe clusterrole <name>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Example output:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"Name:         vm-manager-access\nLabels:       <none>\nAnnotations:  <none>\nPolicyRule:\n  Resources  Non-Resource URLs  Resource Names  Verbs\n  ---------  -----------------  --------------  -----\n  virtualmachines/start, virtualmachines/stop with subresources.kubevirt.io group  []  []  [update]"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a <code>ClusterRoleBinding</code> object to bind the cluster role you have created to the target user or group:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\nkind: ClusterRoleBinding\nmetadata:\n  name: vm-manager-access-binding\nsubjects:\n  - kind: User\n    name: test-user\n    apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io\nroleRef:\n  kind: ClusterRole\n  name: vm-manager-access\n  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io"} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Run the following command to apply the cluster role binding:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f <filename>.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Confirm that the cluster role binding was created by running the following command and observing the output:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get clusterrolebinding <name>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Example output:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                        AGE\nvm-manager-access-binding   15s"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Check if the user can start a VM by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc auth can-i update virtualmachines/start --namespace=<namespace> --as=<user_name> --subresource=subresources.kubevirt.io"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Example output:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"yes"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Check if the user can stop a VM by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc auth can-i update virtualmachines/stop --namespace=<namespace> --as=<user_name> --group=subresources.kubevirt.io"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Example output:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"yes"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
