      </li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="virt-controlling-multiple-vms-web_virt-controlling-vm-states" class="group scroll-mt-20">Controlling the state of multiple virtual machines<a href="#virt-controlling-multiple-vms-web_virt-controlling-vm-states" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">You can start, stop, restart, pause, and unpause multiple virtual machines (VMs) from the web console.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>Navigate to <strong>Virtualization</strong> → <strong>VirtualMachines</strong> in the web console.</li>
      <li>Optional: Enable the <strong>Show only projects with VirtualMachines</strong> option above the tree view to limit the displayed projects.</li>
      <li>Select a relevant project from the tree view.</li>
      <li>
      Navigate to the appropriate menu for your use case:
      <ul>
      <li>
      To change the state of all VMs in the selected project:
      <ol>
      <li>Right-click the name of the project in the tree view and select the intended action from the menu.</li>
      <li>If action confirmation is enabled, confirm the action in the confirmation dialog.</li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      <li>
      To change the state of specific VMs:
      <ol>
      <li>Select a checkbox next to the VMs you want to work with. To select all VMs, click the checkbox in the <strong>VirtualMachines</strong> table header.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Actions</strong> and select the intended action from the menu.</li>
      <li>If action confirmation is enabled, confirm the action in the confirmation dialog.</li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      </ol>
